WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.290
Welcome, everyone, to
CIFAR's series of virtual talks

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.170
and today's conversation
with Stephen Reicher.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.170
These online talks are intended

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.010
to showcase our research
conducted by CIFAR's fellows

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.590
can help inform decision making

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:16.250
as governments develop policies

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.730
to reopen shuttered
societies and economies

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.350
in the wake of COVID-19.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.060
If you're not familiar with us,

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.480
CIFAR is a Canadian-based
global research organization

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.260
that convenes extraordinary minds

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.030
from around the world to address some

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:34.070
of the most important questions
facing science and humanity.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.550
Like many other organizations,

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.270
CIFAR has pivoted quickly
to take action on COVID-19,

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.330
launching new projects and initiatives,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.223
some of which we're highlighting
through this series.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.770
My name is Kate Geddie.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.280
I'm a senior director
of research at CIFAR,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.460
and it's my pleasure to introduce you

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.440
to Professor Stephen Reicher today.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.610
Steve is a professor of psychology

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.550
at the University of St. Andrews

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:04.550
and a leading authority
on social psychology.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.620
His work focuses on
questions of social identity,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.190
collective behavior, crowd
behavior, and leadership.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.130
And he is a fellow

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.160
in CIFAR's new Boundaries,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.210
Membership, &amp; Belonging research program.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.090
In recent months, Steve has served

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.360
on behavioral science advisory
groups to both the Scottish

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.440
and UK governments
concerning the responses

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.650
to the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.380
And despite how busy
that must have kept him,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.270
he has amazingly managed
to coauthor a book

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.700
that was published in late May,

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.147
titled "Together Apart: The
Psychology of COVID-19."

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.880
Given the intense interest
in this topic then and now,

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.640
it was published at that time online

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.760
as an uncorrected proof.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.610
The premise of the book is that,

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:54.040
unless or until a vaccine for
the coronavirus is developed,

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.510
our means of controlling the spread

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.270
of infection depend on behavioral changes

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.840
and hence upon human psychology.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.060
So in today's webinar, Steve will share

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.250
with us insights from this book

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.530
that uses social identity theorizing

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.790
to analyze the psychology
of the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:13.340
such as what encourages
us to come together

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.170
and support one another as a community

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:16.670
to help us through the crisis.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.870
Before we dive in, I'll
quickly set the parameters

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:24.440
for today's session, which
will last 30 minutes.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.150
I'll take my advantage of position as host

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.570
to ask Steve the first few questions

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.260
before opening it up
to questions from you,

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.930
the online participants.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.080
So if you have a
question, please submit it

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.920
through the Q and A function
at the bottom of your screen.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.340
And lastly, I'll mention that this session

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.210
is being recorded and will be available

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.090
on the CIFAR website next week.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.880
So welcome, Steve. Thanks very much.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.120
- Hi.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.860
- To start our conversation,
what is the field

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.070
of social psychology, and why
is it particularly important

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.400
to understand identity and group processes

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.559
under the current pandemic?

00:02:58.559 --> 00:03:02.720
- Okay, well, I'm not sure
whether I'm correcting you

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.220
or correcting myself because
you were citing something

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.510
that I said about behavior being

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:09.980
at the center of this
until we get a vaccine.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.590
Actually, it's gonna be the center of this

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.180
even when we have a vaccine

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.500
because a vaccine does
not stop the infection.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.680
People getting vaccinated does,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.100
and people could be therefore
be willing to be vaccinated.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.650
And the latest polling
evidence I saw showed

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.900
that something like 1/3 of people

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:27.810
are saying they wouldn't
take such a vaccine.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.240
So there's this huge
behavioral question there.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.070
Or equally if we discuss masks, same.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.870
There's a lot of discussion.
Do masks stop the infection?

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:39.980
Well, the masks stop nothing.
Wearing a mask might.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.650
And again, we're talking about behaviors.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.360
We're talking about
whether people wear them,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.250
how they wear them, how
effectively they wear them.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.860
And most obviously, with lockdown,

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.440
lockdown as a policy does nothing

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.470
unless people agree to be locked down.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.620
So right at the core of
every intervention we have,

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.020
including when we have a vaccine,

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:02.930
is dependent upon behavior
and human behavior.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:05.180
Now through this pandemic,

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.530
there have been two very
different approaches

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.770
to human behavior and two ways
of thinking about the place

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.650
of the public in the pandemic response.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.000
One is a very traditional view,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.640
and it's a rather elitist
view, and it's a view that has,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.000
at its core, the notion that human beings

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.450
are essentially psychologically frail.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:30.450
We have a tenuous grasp on
reality. We are full of biases.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.630
We have a tenuous moral compass,

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.660
which is easily thrown aside,

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:41.660
and that fragility is like exacerbated

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:44.380
under conditions A,
where we're in the group,

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.490
the group still further
undermines our rationality,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.430
and secondly when there's a crisis,

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.950
and that's exemplified
by the notion of panic.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:55.163
The notion is that, in
a crisis, people panic.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.500
A Hollywood disaster film
would never be complete

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.490
without a scene of people running away,

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.640
screaming, waving their hands in the air,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:03.810
and clogging up the exit.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.540
So it's human psychology and the frailty

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:11.540
of the human subject which
turns a crisis into a tragedy

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:14.220
and, therefore, a public
or a real problem.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:18.140
Now (chuckles) when you
look more closely at crises,

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.530
actually, that's not what you find.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:23.180
What you find on the whole
is actually people behave

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:25.060
in a fairly restrained way.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.440
They support each other,

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.490
they don't just look after themselves,

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.400
and they are rather resilient.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.527
When it happens, every nation thinks,

00:05:32.527 --> 00:05:33.960
"Oh, there's something special about us

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.860
because we've behaved in a
restrained and a supportive way."

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.320
So in Britain, we like to
think it's the Blitz spirit.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.375
That's what does it. We're
different from the rest of you.

00:05:43.375 --> 00:05:44.208
If you go to New York,

00:05:44.208 --> 00:05:45.820
they'll tell you it's the New York spirit.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.360
You saw it 9/11.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.790
Actually, it happens nearly everywhere.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:54.790
The reason why it happens is
that when we have common fate,

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:58.720
when we face a common
predicament, what we see

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.127
is the emergence of a shared
identity, a sense of us.

00:06:03.127 --> 00:06:06.270
And that sense of us,
that extension of the self

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.610
to include others, has a
number of consequences,

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:13.450
including support for others,
coordination with others,

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:16.640
and therefore with others
working with others

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.910
when others become an asset
to us rather than a problem.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:22.910
Actually, we become
psychologically resilient,

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:26.060
we become empowered, and
we become rather effective.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.930
And what we've seen in this
pandemic in many countries,

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:30.300
certainly we saw it in the UK,

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.940
I think you saw it in Canada as well,

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.120
is that when that shared sense of identity

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.920
is understood, when it is
nurtured by leadership,

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.330
when it is scaffolded by policies

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:47.150
which bring people
together, then actually,

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:51.790
the public develop a sense
of collective resilience,

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:53.370
and that sense of
collective resilience is,

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:57.680
in many ways, the best asset
you have in a pandemic.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.690
And so just to finish this comment,

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.970
for me, if I had a top 10
quotes of the pandemic,

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:07.610
I'm sure there will be lists
like that in Sunday magazines

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.210
in years to come, my number
one would be Andrew Cuomo,

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:12.837
the governor of New York, who said,

00:07:12.837 --> 00:07:15.380
"It's not about I; it's about we.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.100
Get your head around the we concept."

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.560
And what my work has
tried to do for many years

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.867
is to try and understand the we concept,

00:07:22.867 --> 00:07:26.900
I call it social identity
but we concept will do,

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.350
and understand how it can be developed

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:33.210
and harnessed and how the
group becomes the asset

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.999
rather than the problem in a pandemic.

00:07:35.999 --> 00:07:38.430
- Hmm, that's interesting.

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:40.760
Well, I'd like to pick up on the theme

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:42.920
that you mentioned around the role

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.730
of political leaders and suggesting

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.280
that they have a critical role

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.510
in fostering necessary group behaviors

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.790
and dealing with a health emergency.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:52.390
What do we know about what's needed

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:54.690
in terms of effective political leadership

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:58.270
in an emergency situation like a pandemic?

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.220
- Well, let me start with the
concept of leadership itself.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:06.100
And since you plugged the
"Together Apart" book,

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:10.410
also this week, we released
with my colleague Alex Haslam

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:12.150
and Michael Platow, a book on leadership.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.740
So leadership is something
that interests me.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:18.640
And when we look at traditional
notions of leadership,

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:22.580
they are, again, very
hierarchical and very elitist,

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.700
and they start from the premise that

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:28.330
what makes a great leader
is what sets them apart

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.540
from the rest of us.

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:32.253
The great leader has
these amazing qualities,

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:36.680
and if only you could
understand what it was.

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:39.500
If you could isolate this
right stuff and bottle it,

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:41.600
you would solve the problem of leadership.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.300
Now the problem with that,
well, it has many problems.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.160
First of all, it ignores the
fact that leaders, again,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.263
don't achieve anything on their own.

00:08:51.172 --> 00:08:52.420
They achieve it through followers.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:57.420
So leadership which ignores
followers is an empty model.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:03.210
Empirically, that notion
of, it is highly gendered,

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:07.020
great man leadership, has
been around for a long time.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.450
Thomas Carlyle famously in
the, I think it was what,

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:13.270
1848, his book "On heroes
and Hero Worship" said

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:18.140
that history is effectively what great men

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:21.413
have done while they were on Earth.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:25.430
Thousands of studies
trying to find empirically

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.360
what is the quality that shows leadership?

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:30.370
And they've all failed, and they failed

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:31.920
because, actually, they missed the point

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.540
that what makes a great leader
is what actually brings them

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:40.540
together with the group they seek to lead,

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.420
what makes them able to
represent that group,

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:47.703
speak for that group, shape
the understanding of the group.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:51.760
So what the qualities that you need

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.750
in a leader aren't abstract or absolute.

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:57.510
It's more a matter of fit
between our understanding

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:02.260
of who we are and the leaders'
definition of who they are,

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:06.540
and leaders, we call them
entrepreneurs of identity,

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:08.510
are very skilled at telling a narrative

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:11.100
of the group and a narrative of themselves

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.300
which fleshes those two together.

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:16.240
And particularly what
you need in a pandemic

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.250
is you need a sense of shared identity.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:22.800
You need to nurture that sense of usness.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:27.040
You need to bring people
together to support each other

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.530
and actually to do the
practical things but also

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:34.240
to provide the psychological
support to take us through.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:38.760
Now if one of the criteria
for being an effective leader

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:43.230
is to create a unified group,
then we see huge differences

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.330
between different leaders
and different places.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.780
I think the leaders we've
seen who are effective,

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:53.590
so, for instance, Jacinda
Ardern, perhaps, in New Zealand.

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:55.470
Luckily enough, I don't live in England.

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:59.170
I live in Scotland, so we've
seen it with Nicola Sturgeon.

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:00.360
Actually, it's quite interesting.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.830
When you name the leaders,
you see they have something

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:05.770
in common; they tend to be women.

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:08.590
But these are leaders who
have been very effective

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:13.590
in nurturing a sense of us
and in representing that sense

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:18.960
of us and also, of course,
in developing the policies

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.423
which support the
collective self interest.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:25.840
But again, let me make a point here.

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:27.160
It's a simple point, but actually,

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:30.060
it's a rather inconsequential point.

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:32.470
In so much of our social science,

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.710
we start from the premise
that individuals act

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:38.257
in terms of individual self interest.

00:11:38.257 --> 00:11:40.520
And the way to motivate the people

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:44.210
is to tell them what is in
their individual self interest.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:45.880
The problem with that, again,

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.750
is it equates the self
with the individual.

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:51.260
It ignores the fact that
we can define ourself

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.310
in collective terms,
and it ignores the fact

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:56.440
that, actually, in the pandemic,

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:58.750
what is important is we
don't act for ourselves

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:02.350
as individuals, but we
act for the community.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:04.920
So very simply, if people are acting

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:06.400
in terms of individual self interest,

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:08.290
a hell of a lot of people in this pandemic

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:09.400
would save themselves.

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:11.710
Actually, if I do the
cost benefit analyis,

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:13.910
I lose more by staying
in than by going out.

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:15.350
If you're young and you're healthy,

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:17.977
actually, you don't have
much to risk by going out,

00:12:17.977 --> 00:12:20.100
and you lose a hell of
a lot by staying in.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:22.540
I have a 16 year old next door who

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:24.340
is not very happy about having to stay in.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:26.450
He suffers far more than I do.

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:28.460
So on the basis of
individual self interest,

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.750
your calculation would say go out.

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.960
But of course, at a collective level,

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:35.910
if you go out, you spread the disease,

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:38.160
other people get ill, and die.

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.610
So if you want to contain the pandemic,

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:42.950
people need to be thinking of themselves

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:45.510
in terms of that collective identity,

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:47.260
and by and large, that's what happened.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.720
So for instance, I know the UK data,

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:51.550
there may well be data in Canada as well,

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:55.290
but it shows that,
actually, the main factor

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:58.340
which determines whether
people obeyed lockdown or not

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:01.000
was not their sense of individual risk.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.290
It was their sense of communal risk,

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:05.520
their sense of community identity.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:08.920
So again, understanding the we concept,

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:13.330
creating a sense of us, and
representing that sense of us

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:16.870
is absolutely central to leadership.

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:21.810
However, and actually, here, I could try

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:23.950
and show how much I know about Canada,

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:25.220
but I know nothing about Canada.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.728
But luckily, Kate, you-

00:13:27.728 --> 00:13:29.440
- (indistinct), yeah. (laughs)

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:32.400
- I read about Bonnie Henry,
and I loved her quotation

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:35.390
about "we're all in the same storm.

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:37.200
We're not in the same boat."

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.160
And the problem is it's all very well

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.550
to talk the talk of shared identity

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:46.130
and to talk the talk of an
inclusive sense of usness.

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:48.360
But of course, different people

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.510
within the national community are

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:52.420
in very different positions

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:56.880
and are affected very
differently by the pandemic.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:59.560
It's opened up and shone
a spotlight on all that,

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:01.570
all our inequalities.

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:04.930
And therefore, part of
creating a shared identity

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:07.650
is not only to do it rhetorically
but to do it practically.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.800
The practical economic
measures are, for instance,

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:14.830
supporting people so they
can afford to stay at home,

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:18.360
giving money to those who are unemployed,

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:23.150
giving monies to those who
are self employed, and so on.

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:27.310
And only if in practice
you create the conditions

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:31.560
for a shared experience
and a shared response

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.820
will you be able to create
that shared identity.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:36.240
So as I say, it's a matter of rhetoric,

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.230
but it's also a matter of practical policy

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.240
which means that we're not
only in the same storm,

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:44.520
but we are at least to some
extent, in the same boat.

00:14:45.574 --> 00:14:46.690
- Hmm.

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:49.700
Well, now that it's been over four months,

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:52.770
at least in Canada, since the
major COVID-19 restrictions

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:56.800
were put in place, we're
hearing more and more reference

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:59.200
to things like quarantine fatigue.

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:02.360
Is there evidence that
there are potential limits

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:06.200
to our psychological ability
to put up with restrictions?

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:08.900
- You see, this was a huge
controverysy in the UK.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:11.970
And before we locked down,
we had people talking

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:14.010
about behavioral fatigue.

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:19.010
And they actually used
it to justify a delay

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:21.530
in lockdown in the UK, and that, arguably,

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:23.830
that could've cost tens
of thousands of lives.

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:26.950
Depending on how you calculate it,

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:28.670
you've lost about 8,000 lives,

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:29.960
from what I can see, in Canada.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:33.030
We've lost about 60,000.
We've done pretty badly.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:38.030
And this notion of behavioral
fatigue, if it led to a delay,

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:42.190
it's a big issue, okay?

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:46.410
Now when you look at
the notion of fatigue,

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:50.420
it is perfectly true that it is difficult

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:53.720
to sustain lockdown over prolonged period.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:57.880
So the fact that there is
fatigue and compliance decreases,

00:15:57.880 --> 00:15:59.470
I think, is not controversial.

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:02.990
What is controversial is what
is the root of that fatigue?

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:05.367
Is it an essential
psychological fragility?

00:16:05.367 --> 00:16:07.580
And that takes me back
to what I was saying

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:09.940
about different psychological models.

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:11.560
Or is it something else?

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:14.060
So let me give you just one, I think,

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:15.570
very striking piece of evidence.

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:17.590
Again, I apologize, it's British evidence.

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:20.490
But I suspect it's the
same in different places.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:23.480
In the UK, you found a difference

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:26.980
of a factor of six
between the most affluent

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:30.260
and the least affluent in
how often they went out

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:32.681
under lockdown, okay?

00:16:32.681 --> 00:16:34.663
It's a huge difference in compliance.

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:38.180
But when you looked at motivation,

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:40.590
when you looked at the
psychological will to stay in,

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:43.570
there was no difference
between those groups at all.

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:47.030
The difference lay in
just basically the ability

00:16:47.030 --> 00:16:52.030
to stay at home and put food on the table.

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:54.470
So there are a couple of factors

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:57.550
which are absolutely critical
in determining your ability

00:16:57.550 --> 00:16:59.900
to go along with something.

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:02.973
One is, of course, the
information you'll give them.

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.420
People might suffer for a cause,

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:07.080
but if there's no cause to suffer for,

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:08.330
people aren't gonna suffer for it.

00:17:08.330 --> 00:17:10.680
So if the messaging is bad,

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:12.267
and if the messaging suggests to you,

00:17:12.267 --> 00:17:13.910
oh, well, actually, it's all over,

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:16.770
there's no danger, then
people won't comply.

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:18.660
So failures of government messaging,

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.740
we've seen that in the
UK, undermine compliance.

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:23.680
And the second thing is practical.

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:27.030
If people can't afford to stay at home

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:29.550
and feed their families, they won't do so.

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:33.930
So to my mind, the evidence
for the psychological fragility

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:34.900
is actually rather weak.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:39.270
People showed incredible
resilience under lockdown.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:42.230
Again, in the UK, I think it showed

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:43.860
that something like 44% of people

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:46.410
were suffering, but they still stayed in.

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:49.420
I think this has undermined those notions

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:52.140
of fragility and showed
collective resilience

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:53.630
when people worked together.

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:56.810
The problems, as I say, have
been those of messaging,

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:59.210
our government has been very bad at this,

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:01.480
and of giving practical support.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:06.350
And so to talk about fatigue
being a psychological problem

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:07.890
in many ways, for me,

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.350
is the government blaming its own failures

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:12.600
on the failure of human psychology.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:14.400
And I think that's a very dangerous

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:17.340
as well as very unfair thing to do.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:18.867
- Hmm.

00:18:18.867 --> 00:18:20.950
And so as I mentioned at the outset,

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:24.180
you've been advising both the
Scottish and UK governments

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:28.190
on how to encourage adherence
to public health behaviors.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:30.010
In a previous CIFAR webinar,

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:32.840
we had one of our other
fellows, Tim Besley, who's part

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.180
of our Institutions,
Organizations &amp; Growth program,

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:37.440
who spoke to the government use

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:39.563
of research evidence for policymaking.

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:43.860
This seems to be a
particularly political issue

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:45.470
in the UK right now.

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:49.720
Can you tell us why and
how you feel government

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:52.540
should use insights
from academic research,

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:54.300
both to guide the pandemic response

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:57.640
but also a post-pandemic recovery?

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:01.750
- So I think the
responsibility's always around,

00:19:01.750 --> 00:19:05.560
and academics have got to be open

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:08.260
about the limits of what we know

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:11.620
and the certainties of our own evidence.

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:16.400
And clearly, given that COVID-19 is new,

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:19.530
and we're learning things
about it all the time,

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:21.590
both in epidemiological terms but also

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:22.940
in psychological terms,

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:25.070
there are certain things
we couldn't know yet.

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:29.100
We have some general
principles that we could apply,

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:30.680
and there, I think, one of the benefits

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:33.160
of psychological research is that,

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:35.570
on the whole, we do try and understand

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:37.320
like general processes of behavior.

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:40.760
So I can speak, for instance, to the role

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:43.740
of social identities and social norms

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.230
in behavior, and then I can apply that

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:49.330
to the particular types
of identities you draw on,

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.610
all the particular types of norms,

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:54.310
which is necessary to reconfigure
in order to get behavior.

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:56.920
So I think we have to be, as I say,

00:19:56.920 --> 00:19:59.440
open about our own limitations.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:01.540
And then from the
perspective of government,

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:03.610
I think there are two pitfalls,

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:07.320
one being to ignore the science

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:08.710
because the science, of course,

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:10.810
can tell you what the consequence

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:13.240
of particular actions will be.

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:16.200
But the other error, and I
think it's equally problematic,

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.860
is to claim to be led by the science

00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:20.600
because if the science can tell you

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:23.440
what particular outcomes are likely to be,

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:27.030
what it can't do is tell you how much risk

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:28.970
should you or shouldn't you take

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:33.680
or how do you balance different
risks against each other?

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:35.410
Because that's a political question

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:37.650
or a value question, if you like.

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:40.140
And to claim that you
are led by the science

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:42.820
can sometimes hide the political choices

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:45.410
which I think is dangerous
and disingenuous.

00:20:45.410 --> 00:20:48.150
So I think it's very important
to get the balance right

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:50.800
and to say the government
should listen to us

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:53.910
and governments should be
informed by the science.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.790
But government should not
be led by the science.

00:20:56.790 --> 00:20:59.650
And if they claim that they
are led by the science,

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:01.140
what they're trying to do is

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.570
to hide their own political choices

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:06.710
and therefore their
political accountability

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:07.810
behind the science.

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:09.410
And then, of course, what comes next

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:12.740
when things have gone wrong
is to blame the science

00:21:12.740 --> 00:21:14.960
for the political choices that they made,

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:18.160
and I see signs of that
happening in the UK.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:19.540
- Hmm.

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:21.330
Well, thank you so much, Steve,

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:23.240
for sharing your insights with us.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:25.730
We've got just, I think, a
little under 10 minutes left,

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:27.340
so I'm gonna try and get
some of the questions

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:29.140
that have come in from our audience.

00:21:34.753 --> 00:21:36.380
So the person has written in

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.493
that they find the most
challenging parts of COVID-19

00:21:38.493 --> 00:21:40.310
is that the effects are delayed,

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:42.010
that there's a two-week delay period

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:43.450
between infection and symptoms,

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:44.830
and it's hard to know which types

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:47.213
of personal protective
measures are effective.

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:51.440
How has human psychology
adapted to dealing

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:54.682
with this kind of delay between
actions and consequences?

00:21:54.682 --> 00:21:56.320
- Hmm, yeah.

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:58.163
Well, first of all, of course,

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:03.420
the asymptomatic transmission
issue we've discovered

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:07.587
is one of the most
significant issues of COVID,

00:22:07.587 --> 00:22:11.810
and it's what makes it actually
such a dangerous disease

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:14.680
because a lot of people who don't know

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:19.680
that they are ill are going
out and shedding the virus.

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:21.400
That's one of the reasons.

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:23.820
People thought the major parameters were,

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:26.920
if you like, how infectious it was

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:28.390
and how many people it killed.

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:31.603
But the delay is critical
to the epidemiology.

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:34.520
It's also critical to issues like

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:36.210
why we should wears masks because,

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:38.270
a lot of the time, we
could spread the disease

00:22:38.270 --> 00:22:42.493
without knowing that we
are spreading the disease.

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:47.640
There is a lot of research in psychology

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:50.610
about delayed gratification and

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:53.280
about temporality and so on.

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:57.100
I think also, again, actually,
when it comes down to

00:22:57.100 --> 00:23:02.100
is clarity about understanding
and clarity about messaging.

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:05.680
And it comes back to, actually,
an even broader issue.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:10.680
If you want to ask how
are authorities affected

00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:13.623
and why people comply with authorities,

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:16.220
the literature tends to suggest

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:18.740
that it's not to do with
authorities being able

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:21.620
to punish you but you having a sense

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:25.250
of the authorities acting for you

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:28.480
and the authorities being on your side.

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:30.520
And so work on procedural justice says

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:33.560
if you want people to go
along with authorities,

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:37.230
in many ways, treat them
as you would a member

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:38.210
of your own group.

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:40.160
Treat them as a partner.

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:44.450
And part of doing that means
being open with people.

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:46.380
It means respecting people.

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:49.890
It means giving people all the
information that they need.

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:52.580
And one of the dangers
of this fragility notion

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:54.630
is that if you give people
too much information,

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:57.180
they'll panic, so we will
hide the complexities

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:59.460
from people 'cause they're not up to it.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:02.030
My sense is that people are fully able

00:24:02.030 --> 00:24:04.900
to understand these things as long

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:09.140
as a government has an adult
conversation with people,

00:24:09.140 --> 00:24:13.490
respects people, and
provides a clear mental model

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:15.890
of the nature of the disease.

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:18.560
And so to me, this is
very much not an issue

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:21.180
of psychological limitations.

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:22.920
It's an issue of the problems

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:25.730
of the psychological government's models

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:27.490
which authority has,

00:24:27.490 --> 00:24:30.580
and it's an issue of
clarity of communication,

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:33.160
giving us a clear mental model.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:36.100
And part of that mental
model is an understanding

00:24:36.100 --> 00:24:39.930
that we've got to act as
if we've got the virus,

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:41.430
even when we haven't.

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:43.330
And we've got to take the precautions

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:47.780
as if we could have the
virus, even when we don't.

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:49.180
And so I think those forms

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:53.370
of communication become an
absolutely central issue.

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:54.850
- Hmm.

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:57.650
Do you think there'll be a lasting effect

00:24:57.650 --> 00:24:59.070
on social interactions even

00:24:59.070 --> 00:25:01.770
after the vaccine's
developed and deployed?

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:04.400
- See, the paradox of that question,

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:06.980
I think, is, so there's all this debate

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:10.210
about how will society change.

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:12.820
Well, the problem is that if you think

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:15.600
that society's gonna inevitably change

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:19.180
in particular ways, you don't
fight for those changes.

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:22.100
You become passive, which
means that they're less likely

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:24.210
to happen, so that's the paradox.

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:26.690
Paradox is the more you
say this is gonna happen,

00:25:26.690 --> 00:25:28.670
the less likely it is to happen.

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:31.210
For me, I look at it slightly differently.

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:36.210
I say, "What has this pandemic shown us?"

00:25:36.410 --> 00:25:40.150
And I think it's shown
us two very clear things.

00:25:40.150 --> 00:25:43.260
First of all, of course,
it's shone a spotlight

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:48.260
on inequalities, certainly
of race and ethnicity,

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.830
certainly of class, certainly of gender,

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:52.450
of generation as well.

00:25:52.450 --> 00:25:55.490
It has shown us how pernicious they are.

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:57.770
It has shown us how murderous they are,

00:25:57.770 --> 00:25:59.040
things that people knew before,

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:01.630
but it's become blindingly obvious.

00:26:01.630 --> 00:26:04.220
And it's also shown us that the response

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:07.420
when people are angry
at those inequalities

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:10.570
is not repression; that simply
leads to social disorder.

00:26:10.570 --> 00:26:15.570
It's to acknowledge,
address, and to change.

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:18.770
And so I hope that that vision,

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:22.740
both of the inequalities
and of the inadequacy

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:25.560
of a repressive response, will empower us

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:29.010
to struggle for a more equitable society.

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:32.237
The second thing it's shown
us in the (indistinct)

00:26:32.237 --> 00:26:34.520
that's been at the core
of what I've been saying

00:26:34.520 --> 00:26:39.520
is it changes our understanding
of the relationship

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:41.930
between the individual and the collective

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:43.660
in a quite profound way.

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:46.280
We've always been told the individual

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:49.620
is relatively rational, and
the group is bad for us.

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:51.110
So it's an idea that came out

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:52.840
in the period of industrialization,

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:55.240
basically because of
elite fear of the masses.

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:59.730
What we've seen in this pandemic
is the power of the group.

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:02.830
Now of course, that power
can be used in bad ways,

00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:05.030
and we see that in pandemics.

00:27:05.030 --> 00:27:08.410
At times, we see prejudice.
We see pogroms in pandemics.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:12.310
But it can be used
positively like dynamite.

00:27:12.310 --> 00:27:15.530
It can be a destructive power.
It can be constructive power.

00:27:15.530 --> 00:27:19.251
I think understanding the
power of the collective

00:27:19.251 --> 00:27:21.400
and the fact that the
collective is not less rational

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:24.500
than the individual is a profound change

00:27:24.500 --> 00:27:27.460
for our social sciences,
and it raises questions,

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:28.770
of course, about the relationship

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:31.080
between the individual and the state.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:32.700
And perhaps the state needs

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:35.760
to abandon its paternalistic
notion that people

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:38.680
are irrational and needs
the state to look after them

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:41.650
and change it to, if you like,
a notion that the state has

00:27:41.650 --> 00:27:45.370
to scaffold the collective
self organization of people.

00:27:45.370 --> 00:27:46.730
It's not a cheap option.

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:49.130
It's an option for budget cuts because,

00:27:49.130 --> 00:27:51.863
actually, self organization
requires resources.

00:27:53.232 --> 00:27:54.720
It requires funds.

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:55.940
But certainly, I think there

00:27:55.940 --> 00:28:00.050
are some profound visions
we've had, but they are visions

00:28:00.050 --> 00:28:02.090
that give us a notion
of what we want to get.

00:28:02.090 --> 00:28:03.950
They're not gonna happen automatically,

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:06.640
and they're things that are
gonna have to be fought for.

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:07.473
- Hmm.

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:11.287
I'm looking at time, and trying to decide

00:28:11.287 --> 00:28:12.770
if I can squeeze in one more question.

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:17.480
And it relates to the fact that
we often, we probably, well,

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:20.530
we all likely have
multiple overlapping kinds

00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:22.750
of group identity and one of which

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:26.203
may be affiliation with
different political parties.

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:29.140
Particularly in Canada,
the US, and the UK,

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:31.870
political party affiliation

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:34.590
can be quite a strong element of identity.

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:37.800
Are there opportunities here
to strengthen solidarity

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:42.800
or to improve adherence
to public health measures

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:44.320
along political party lines?

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:48.173
- Here, again, I think leadership
is absolutely critical.

00:28:48.173 --> 00:28:51.150
You can have polarizing leadership,

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:55.040
and, in fact, to me, what's
happened in the United States

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:56.520
is quite extraordinary.

00:28:56.520 --> 00:28:59.760
One has a president who positions himself

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:02.250
as the anti-political president

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:07.160
and who is as an insurgent even in office.

00:29:07.160 --> 00:29:09.870
The office of the president
of the United States acts

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:13.210
as an insurgent and
encourages people to act

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:18.090
in anti-state ways, encourages
people to violate lockdown,

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:21.180
encourages people to
protest and even be violent

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:23.520
against those who have taken
public health measures.

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:27.990
You can have a deeply
polarizing leadership.

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:29.960
I think you've seen it in
other countries as well,

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:32.900
so I think, for instance, in India,

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:36.320
we saw it to some extent in
the ways in which it was used

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:40.550
to pursue a Hindu nationalistic
agenda and to talk,

00:29:40.550 --> 00:29:44.190
for instance, about corona
Jihadism, to blame Muslims.

00:29:44.190 --> 00:29:48.760
So I think bad leadership in a pandemic

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:53.760
is really the worst of all
worlds and can divide people.

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:56.860
But where there has been
good inclusive leadership,

00:29:56.860 --> 00:29:58.970
as I say, which goes
along with the measures

00:29:58.970 --> 00:30:00.850
that bring people together, then yes,

00:30:00.850 --> 00:30:02.800
I think we've seen extraordinary levels

00:30:06.016 --> 00:30:09.760
of unity and extraordinary
levels of support.

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:11.950
So again, we sometimes think

00:30:11.950 --> 00:30:15.920
of contexts automatically
changing human behavior.

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:17.920
No, it's the way in which contexts

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:20.550
are understood and made sense of.

00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:24.470
It's the interaction between
the structure of experience

00:30:24.470 --> 00:30:26.350
and leadership and structure of rhetoric,

00:30:26.350 --> 00:30:27.690
which determines outcomes.

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:31.080
It's not inevitable.
It's not deterministic.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.420
It's the intersection of the political

00:30:33.420 --> 00:30:36.020
and the psychological which
answers those questions.

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:40.260
- Well, that is all the time

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:43.370
that we have for today, unfortunately.

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:45.450
Thank you to all who are
joining us from home,

00:30:45.450 --> 00:30:47.840
and a huge thank you to
you, Stephen Reicher,

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:50.310
for sharing your insights with us.

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:52.580
Please visit our website at CIFAR.ca

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.810
to find out more about the series

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:56.500
and to view a recording of this talk,

00:30:56.500 --> 00:30:58.710
which should be posted early next week.

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:00.820
- Could I make one final, final point,

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:01.653
- Please.
- if I may,

00:31:01.653 --> 00:31:04.293
which is that the book which we wrote,

00:31:07.046 --> 00:31:09.770
we wrote in order to be as
broadly available as possible.

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:12.510
So it is available as a free download,

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:13.343
doesn't cost you anything.

00:31:13.343 --> 00:31:16.410
You can buy it as a paperback,
but it's a free download.

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:19.320
So if you go to look up "Together Apart,"

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:22.200
that's the name of the book,
you can have it for free,

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:24.290
and you can see some of the literature

00:31:24.290 --> 00:31:26.090
and the evidence that lies behind some

00:31:26.090 --> 00:31:27.630
of the things I've been saying.

00:31:27.630 --> 00:31:29.832
- [Kate] Wonderful. Thanks so much, Steve.

00:31:29.832 --> 00:31:31.665
- [Stephen] Thank you.

